const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"RC_E4_001","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P1","passage":"A growing body of research says that presenteeism – the problem of workers' being on the job but, because of some medical conditions, not fully functioning – can cut individual productivity by one-third or more. In fact, presenteeism appears to be a costlier problem than its productivity-reducing counterpart, absenteeism. And, unlike absenteeism, presenteeism isn't always apparent: you cannot tell when – or how much – a medical condition is hindering someone's performance.\\n\\nHowever, a handful of companies are recognizing the problem and trying to do something about it. That entails determining the prevalence of health conditions that undermine job performance, calculating the related productivity loss, and combating that loss in cost-effective ways. This is a new area of study, and questions remain around a host of issues, including the central one: the exact degree to which various illnesses reduce productivity.\\n\\nPresenteeism, as defined by researchers, isn't about malingering (pretending to be ill to avoid work duties) or goofing off on the job (surfing the Internet, say, when you should be preparing a report). The term refers to productivity loss resulting from real health problems. Underlying the research on presenteeism is the assumption that employees do not take their jobs lightly, that most of them need and want to continue working if they can.\\n\\nResearchers have also tried to quantify the impact of disease in general on workplace productivity. Using the same methodology employed to gauge the cost of depression and pain, a research team calculated the total cost of presenteeism in the United States to be more than $150 billion per year. Furthermore, most studies confirm that presenteeism is costlier than illness-related absenteeism or disability. That is, less time was actually lost from people staying home than from them showing up but not performing at the top of their game.\\n\\nWhat may be more significant – but is also controversial – is that presenteeism appears to cost companies substantially more than they spend directly on medical treatment and drugs. (It's important to note that many presenteeism studies, though conducted by academics or health management consultants, are proposed and funded by pharmaceutical companies hoping to show that certain medications are worth paying for because they will increase worker productivity by ameliorating symptoms of illness.) Typically, studies show that presenteeism costs employees two to three times more than direct medical care, which is paid for by companies in the form of insurance premiums or employee claims.\\n\\nBut such findings, while striking, are academic until a company takes a close look at the effects of illness on productivity of its own workforce. Bank One, for instance, has calculated its direct and indirect health costs and found that the direct spending represents only a fraction of the company's total costs.","question":"Which of the following best states the author's main point?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Present studies conclusively prove that presenteeism is a far higher cost than is absenteeism.","B":"The assertion of current wisdom is that presenteeism, despite its intangible nature, can be accurately measured.","C":"Though tentative, studies show that presenteeism is a more significant cost to companies than expected.","D":"As per the recent studies, presenteeism is likely to affect the profitability of companies if adequate health measures are not taken up."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Though tentative, studies show that presenteeism is a more significant cost to companies than expected.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"too_extreme","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect because the passage does not state the studies to be conclusive — 'too_extreme' because the student amplifies the strength of findings beyond what the passage claims. Choice B is incorrect because the passage openly declares that presenteeism is not always apparent, implying it cannot be accurately measured — this contradicts the passage directly. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage discusses studies that suggest presenteeism is a significant cost while acknowledging the research is new and questions remain. Choice D is incorrect because the passage does not discuss the effect of presenteeism on profitability — this introduces a consequence not mentioned in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_002","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P1","passage":"A growing body of research says that presenteeism – the problem of workers' being on the job but, because of some medical conditions, not fully functioning – can cut individual productivity by one-third or more. In fact, presenteeism appears to be a costlier problem than its productivity-reducing counterpart, absenteeism. And, unlike absenteeism, presenteeism isn't always apparent: you cannot tell when – or how much – a medical condition is hindering someone's performance.\\n\\nHowever, a handful of companies are recognizing the problem and trying to do something about it. That entails determining the prevalence of health conditions that undermine job performance, calculating the related productivity loss, and combating that loss in cost-effective ways. This is a new area of study, and questions remain around a host of issues, including the central one: the exact degree to which various illnesses reduce productivity.\\n\\nPresenteeism, as defined by researchers, isn't about malingering (pretending to be ill to avoid work duties) or goofing off on the job (surfing the Internet, say, when you should be preparing a report). The term refers to productivity loss resulting from real health problems. Underlying the research on presenteeism is the assumption that employees do not take their jobs lightly, that most of them need and want to continue working if they can.\\n\\nResearchers have also tried to quantify the impact of disease in general on workplace productivity. Using the same methodology employed to gauge the cost of depression and pain, a research team calculated the total cost of presenteeism in the United States to be more than $150 billion per year. Furthermore, most studies confirm that presenteeism is costlier than illness-related absenteeism or disability. That is, less time was actually lost from people staying home than from them showing up but not performing at the top of their game.\\n\\nWhat may be more significant – but is also controversial – is that presenteeism appears to cost companies substantially more than they spend directly on medical treatment and drugs. (It's important to note that many presenteeism studies, though conducted by academics or health management consultants, are proposed and funded by pharmaceutical companies hoping to show that certain medications are worth paying for because they will increase worker productivity by ameliorating symptoms of illness.) Typically, studies show that presenteeism costs employees two to three times more than direct medical care, which is paid for by companies in the form of insurance premiums or employee claims.\\n\\nBut such findings, while striking, are academic until a company takes a close look at the effects of illness on productivity of its own workforce. Bank One, for instance, has calculated its direct and indirect health costs and found that the direct spending represents only a fraction of the company's total costs.","question":"According to the passage, the estimation of cost resulting from presenteeism is likely to be controversial because","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the definition of presenteeism is fluid and open to both question and correction because of the intangible nature of the concept.","B":"the studies on presenteeism arrived at different values for the cost incurred by presenteeism, making consensus impossible.","C":"only a handful of companies now recognize the problem and try to arrive at a solution for the problems of presenteeism.","D":"the organizations funding the studies on presenteeism are business entities with questionable commitment to academic advancement."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"the organizations funding the studies on presenteeism are business entities with questionable commitment to academic advancement.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"out_of_scope","C":"irrelevant_comparison"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — while the intangibility of presenteeism is mentioned, the passage does not cite this as the specific reason for the controversy around cost estimates; this is plausible but unstated in this context. Choice B is incorrect — the passage does not mention different studies arriving at different values. Choice C is incorrect — the number of companies recognising the problem is not related to why cost estimates are controversial. Choice D is CORRECT — the fifth paragraph explicitly states studies are 'proposed and funded by pharmaceutical companies hoping to show that certain medications are worth paying for', implying biased economic interest rather than unbiased academic interest.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_003","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P1","passage":"A growing body of research says that presenteeism – the problem of workers' being on the job but, because of some medical conditions, not fully functioning – can cut individual productivity by one-third or more. In fact, presenteeism appears to be a costlier problem than its productivity-reducing counterpart, absenteeism. And, unlike absenteeism, presenteeism isn't always apparent: you cannot tell when – or how much – a medical condition is hindering someone's performance.\\n\\nHowever, a handful of companies are recognizing the problem and trying to do something about it. That entails determining the prevalence of health conditions that undermine job performance, calculating the related productivity loss, and combating that loss in cost-effective ways. This is a new area of study, and questions remain around a host of issues, including the central one: the exact degree to which various illnesses reduce productivity.\\n\\nPresenteeism, as defined by researchers, isn't about malingering (pretending to be ill to avoid work duties) or goofing off on the job (surfing the Internet, say, when you should be preparing a report). The term refers to productivity loss resulting from real health problems. Underlying the research on presenteeism is the assumption that employees do not take their jobs lightly, that most of them need and want to continue working if they can.\\n\\nResearchers have also tried to quantify the impact of disease in general on workplace productivity. Using the same methodology employed to gauge the cost of depression and pain, a research team calculated the total cost of presenteeism in the United States to be more than $150 billion per year. Furthermore, most studies confirm that presenteeism is costlier than illness-related absenteeism or disability. That is, less time was actually lost from people staying home than from them showing up but not performing at the top of their game.\\n\\nWhat may be more significant – but is also controversial – is that presenteeism appears to cost companies substantially more than they spend directly on medical treatment and drugs. (It's important to note that many presenteeism studies, though conducted by academics or health management consultants, are proposed and funded by pharmaceutical companies hoping to show that certain medications are worth paying for because they will increase worker productivity by ameliorating symptoms of illness.) Typically, studies show that presenteeism costs employees two to three times more than direct medical care, which is paid for by companies in the form of insurance premiums or employee claims.\\n\\nBut such findings, while striking, are academic until a company takes a close look at the effects of illness on productivity of its own workforce. Bank One, for instance, has calculated its direct and indirect health costs and found that the direct spending represents only a fraction of the company's total costs.","question":"The author uses the word 'academic' in the last paragraph to imply that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the studies concluding about the presenteeism costs exaggerate the actual costs incurred during its existence.","B":"the presenteeism costs being only a fraction of a company's total cost, they cannot significantly influence the profits of that company.","C":"in the current stage, the cost estimates for presenteeism are more of theoretical interest than of empirical interest.","D":"the direct costs of a company in providing health care are likely to be less than those of presenteeism."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"in the current stage, the cost estimates for presenteeism are more of theoretical interest than of empirical interest.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — 'academic' in this context does not imply exaggeration; the word signals theoretical rather than practical relevance. Choice B is incorrect — the passage states direct spending is a fraction of total costs, meaning presenteeism costs are large, not negligible; this contradicts the passage. Choice C is CORRECT — 'academic' here means 'of theoretical interest only', implying findings are not yet practically actionable until a company examines its own workforce. Choice D is incorrect — the passage states the reverse: presenteeism costs more than direct medical care.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_004","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P1","passage":"A growing body of research says that presenteeism – the problem of workers' being on the job but, because of some medical conditions, not fully functioning – can cut individual productivity by one-third or more. In fact, presenteeism appears to be a costlier problem than its productivity-reducing counterpart, absenteeism. And, unlike absenteeism, presenteeism isn't always apparent: you cannot tell when – or how much – a medical condition is hindering someone's performance.\\n\\nHowever, a handful of companies are recognizing the problem and trying to do something about it. That entails determining the prevalence of health conditions that undermine job performance, calculating the related productivity loss, and combating that loss in cost-effective ways. This is a new area of study, and questions remain around a host of issues, including the central one: the exact degree to which various illnesses reduce productivity.\\n\\nPresenteeism, as defined by researchers, isn't about malingering (pretending to be ill to avoid work duties) or goofing off on the job (surfing the Internet, say, when you should be preparing a report). The term refers to productivity loss resulting from real health problems. Underlying the research on presenteeism is the assumption that employees do not take their jobs lightly, that most of them need and want to continue working if they can.\\n\\nResearchers have also tried to quantify the impact of disease in general on workplace productivity. Using the same methodology employed to gauge the cost of depression and pain, a research team calculated the total cost of presenteeism in the United States to be more than $150 billion per year. Furthermore, most studies confirm that presenteeism is costlier than illness-related absenteeism or disability. That is, less time was actually lost from people staying home than from them showing up but not performing at the top of their game.\\n\\nWhat may be more significant – but is also controversial – is that presenteeism appears to cost companies substantially more than they spend directly on medical treatment and drugs. (It's important to note that many presenteeism studies, though conducted by academics or health management consultants, are proposed and funded by pharmaceutical companies hoping to show that certain medications are worth paying for because they will increase worker productivity by ameliorating symptoms of illness.) Typically, studies show that presenteeism costs employees two to three times more than direct medical care, which is paid for by companies in the form of insurance premiums or employee claims.\\n\\nBut such findings, while striking, are academic until a company takes a close look at the effects of illness on productivity of its own workforce. Bank One, for instance, has calculated its direct and indirect health costs and found that the direct spending represents only a fraction of the company's total costs.","question":"As per the information given in the passage, we can expect that the significance of presenteeism prevention measures can become economically questionable for the company, if","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"employees who are benefited from the presenteeism prevention measures are likely to be loyal to the company.","B":"companies have to spend a substantial portion of their total cost on direct health care for their employees.","C":"small amounts to be spent on more number of employees to prevent presenteeism can accrue to amounts far more than the amount to be spent on direct medical care for fewer number of employees.","D":"the definition of presenteeism and its quantification are questionable under the present state of the development of the concept."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"small amounts to be spent on more number of employees to prevent presenteeism can accrue to amounts far more than the amount to be spent on direct medical care for fewer number of employees.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — employee loyalty is not mentioned in the passage as a factor in the economic case for presenteeism prevention. Choice B is incorrect — if companies already spend heavily on direct health care, that would make presenteeism prevention relatively more attractive, not less; this contradicts the logic of the question. Choice C is CORRECT — if prevention costs per employee, when scaled across a large workforce, exceed the savings relative to direct medical care, the measures become economically unjustifiable. Choice D is incorrect — while the conceptual difficulty is a limitation, it is not an economic disadvantage per se; this is a methodological concern, not a cost-benefit one.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_005","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P1","passage":"A growing body of research says that presenteeism – the problem of workers' being on the job but, because of some medical conditions, not fully functioning – can cut individual productivity by one-third or more. In fact, presenteeism appears to be a costlier problem than its productivity-reducing counterpart, absenteeism. And, unlike absenteeism, presenteeism isn't always apparent: you cannot tell when – or how much – a medical condition is hindering someone's performance.\\n\\nHowever, a handful of companies are recognizing the problem and trying to do something about it. That entails determining the prevalence of health conditions that undermine job performance, calculating the related productivity loss, and combating that loss in cost-effective ways. This is a new area of study, and questions remain around a host of issues, including the central one: the exact degree to which various illnesses reduce productivity.\\n\\nPresenteeism, as defined by researchers, isn't about malingering (pretending to be ill to avoid work duties) or goofing off on the job (surfing the Internet, say, when you should be preparing a report). The term refers to productivity loss resulting from real health problems. Underlying the research on presenteeism is the assumption that employees do not take their jobs lightly, that most of them need and want to continue working if they can.\\n\\nResearchers have also tried to quantify the impact of disease in general on workplace productivity. Using the same methodology employed to gauge the cost of depression and pain, a research team calculated the total cost of presenteeism in the United States to be more than $150 billion per year. Furthermore, most studies confirm that presenteeism is costlier than illness-related absenteeism or disability. That is, less time was actually lost from people staying home than from them showing up but not performing at the top of their game.\\n\\nWhat may be more significant – but is also controversial – is that presenteeism appears to cost companies substantially more than they spend directly on medical treatment and drugs. (It's important to note that many presenteeism studies, though conducted by academics or health management consultants, are proposed and funded by pharmaceutical companies hoping to show that certain medications are worth paying for because they will increase worker productivity by ameliorating symptoms of illness.) Typically, studies show that presenteeism costs employees two to three times more than direct medical care, which is paid for by companies in the form of insurance premiums or employee claims.\\n\\nBut such findings, while striking, are academic until a company takes a close look at the effects of illness on productivity of its own workforce. Bank One, for instance, has calculated its direct and indirect health costs and found that the direct spending represents only a fraction of the company's total costs.","question":"According to the passage, a company trying to minimize presenteeism costs should do all of the following EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"identifying medical problems that reduce worker productivity.","B":"clearly quantifying the amount lost due to presenteeism.","C":"allotting funds for the prevention of presenteeism in a judicial way.","D":"offering more direct health care for its employees."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"offering more direct health care for its employees.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choices A, B, and C are all directly stated in the second paragraph as the required actions: 'determining the prevalence of health conditions that undermine job performance, calculating the related productivity loss, and combating that loss in cost-effective ways.' Students who pick these confuse actions the passage endorses with actions the passage does not recommend — they restate the premise. Choice D is CORRECT — increasing direct health care is not listed as a presenteeism prevention measure; the passage treats direct medical costs as a separate and smaller cost category, not a solution to presenteeism.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E4_006","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P2","passage":"Christianity attempted to suggest that the Universe is a theatre of change and imperfection; and at the same time to teach that God is perfect and unchanging. Those men who sought to reconcile Christianity with Greek philosophy were known as Apologists. They taught that the universe contains traces of something different from pure matter and thus points to a God who is eternal, unchanging and good. This God is the first cause of everything in the Universe, the creator of the Universe. God is the eternal and abiding principle in all change, the eternal pattern which never changes. He is the unity of all forms, all ideas. Through divine emanations, He has created the world and everything in the world, in so far it is a part of God, strives to be more like God and try to return to Him. The creator fashioned the world from matter which He created out of nothing.\\n\\nOne of the greatest thinkers among these early Christian philosophers, one who worked out the theory of the Apologists most completely was Saint Augustine. God, he taught, created matter out of nothing and then created everything in the Universe. The forms which He impressed upon matter were in God's mind from the beginning of time. Indeed, God created time and space also. Thus, everything that is or ever shall be is a creation of God and must follow His laws and will.\\n\\nHere again, we see the influence of the Greeks in the belief that the Universe is the result of the coming together of matter and form.\\n\\nBut the Christian thinkers went further than the Greeks in that they attempted to account for the existence of matter. You will remember that the Greeks simply accepted matter as well as the ideas or forms as existing from the beginning. The Christians put the ideas or forms in God's mind and went on to say that God created matter out of nothing. After He had created matter, He had something upon which to impress ideas or forms.\\n\\nFurther, these Christian philosophers taught that the ideas or forms, being in the mind of God, were divine. Therefore, in so far as things are ideas or forms impressed on matter, they seek God and try to return to Him. But matter holds them back. Matter which God has created is the principle which makes it necessary for things to struggle in their attempt to become divine.\\n\\nAugustine lived during the fourth century of the Christian era. He saw the great Roman Empire which had been established by Caesar falling to pieces and watched the barbarians from the north gradually moving down into the empire and even toward Rome.","question":"Which of the following can be a part of what St. Augustine would have taught?\\n\\na. All creations of God will follow His laws and will.\\nb. God created matter out of nothing.\\nc. God impressed forms upon matter to create everything in the universe.\\nd. There is nothing that can be constant in this universe – even time, space and matter.","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"a and b","B":"b and d","C":"b, c and d","D":"a, b and c"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"a, b and c","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Statement a — Augustine taught that everything is a creation of God and must follow His laws and will (second paragraph, last sentence); TRUE. Statement b — Augustine taught that God created matter out of nothing (second paragraph); TRUE. Statement c — Augustine taught that God impressed forms upon matter to create everything (second paragraph); TRUE. Statement d — Augustine taught that God created time and space, not that nothing is constant; this misrepresents his position; FALSE. Therefore a, b and c are correct — Choice D. Choices A, B and C either omit a valid statement or include the false statement d.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_007","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P2","passage":"Christianity attempted to suggest that the Universe is a theatre of change and imperfection; and at the same time to teach that God is perfect and unchanging. Those men who sought to reconcile Christianity with Greek philosophy were known as Apologists. They taught that the universe contains traces of something different from pure matter and thus points to a God who is eternal, unchanging and good. This God is the first cause of everything in the Universe, the creator of the Universe. God is the eternal and abiding principle in all change, the eternal pattern which never changes. He is the unity of all forms, all ideas. Through divine emanations, He has created the world and everything in the world, in so far it is a part of God, strives to be more like God and try to return to Him. The creator fashioned the world from matter which He created out of nothing.\\n\\nOne of the greatest thinkers among these early Christian philosophers, one who worked out the theory of the Apologists most completely was Saint Augustine. God, he taught, created matter out of nothing and then created everything in the Universe. The forms which He impressed upon matter were in God's mind from the beginning of time. Indeed, God created time and space also. Thus, everything that is or ever shall be is a creation of God and must follow His laws and will.\\n\\nHere again, we see the influence of the Greeks in the belief that the Universe is the result of the coming together of matter and form.\\n\\nBut the Christian thinkers went further than the Greeks in that they attempted to account for the existence of matter. You will remember that the Greeks simply accepted matter as well as the ideas or forms as existing from the beginning. The Christians put the ideas or forms in God's mind and went on to say that God created matter out of nothing. After He had created matter, He had something upon which to impress ideas or forms.\\n\\nFurther, these Christian philosophers taught that the ideas or forms, being in the mind of God, were divine. Therefore, in so far as things are ideas or forms impressed on matter, they seek God and try to return to Him. But matter holds them back. Matter which God has created is the principle which makes it necessary for things to struggle in their attempt to become divine.\\n\\nAugustine lived during the fourth century of the Christian era. He saw the great Roman Empire which had been established by Caesar falling to pieces and watched the barbarians from the north gradually moving down into the empire and even toward Rome.","question":"Regarding Greek philosophy, choose an alternative that is true as per the passage.","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Greek philosophers might not have attempted to account for matter while explaining the creation of universe.","B":"It says that the universe is the manifestation of interplay between change and imperfection.","C":"It strongly believes that God is the eternal unchanging good.","D":"All the above"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"All the above","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A is true — the passage states Greeks 'simply accepted matter as well as the ideas or forms as existing from the beginning', meaning they did not account for matter's origin. Choice B is true — the passage opens with 'the Universe is a theatre of change and imperfection', a core idea also present in Greek-influenced thinking. Choice C is true — the Apologists drew from Greek philosophy to describe God as 'eternal, unchanging and good'. Since all three are individually supported, Choice D is correct. Students who pick A, B or C individually fail to check whether the other statements also hold.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_008","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P2","passage":"Christianity attempted to suggest that the Universe is a theatre of change and imperfection; and at the same time to teach that God is perfect and unchanging. Those men who sought to reconcile Christianity with Greek philosophy were known as Apologists. They taught that the universe contains traces of something different from pure matter and thus points to a God who is eternal, unchanging and good. This God is the first cause of everything in the Universe, the creator of the Universe. God is the eternal and abiding principle in all change, the eternal pattern which never changes. He is the unity of all forms, all ideas. Through divine emanations, He has created the world and everything in the world, in so far it is a part of God, strives to be more like God and try to return to Him. The creator fashioned the world from matter which He created out of nothing.\\n\\nOne of the greatest thinkers among these early Christian philosophers, one who worked out the theory of the Apologists most completely was Saint Augustine. God, he taught, created matter out of nothing and then created everything in the Universe. The forms which He impressed upon matter were in God's mind from the beginning of time. Indeed, God created time and space also. Thus, everything that is or ever shall be is a creation of God and must follow His laws and will.\\n\\nHere again, we see the influence of the Greeks in the belief that the Universe is the result of the coming together of matter and form.\\n\\nBut the Christian thinkers went further than the Greeks in that they attempted to account for the existence of matter. You will remember that the Greeks simply accepted matter as well as the ideas or forms as existing from the beginning. The Christians put the ideas or forms in God's mind and went on to say that God created matter out of nothing. After He had created matter, He had something upon which to impress ideas or forms.\\n\\nFurther, these Christian philosophers taught that the ideas or forms, being in the mind of God, were divine. Therefore, in so far as things are ideas or forms impressed on matter, they seek God and try to return to Him. But matter holds them back. Matter which God has created is the principle which makes it necessary for things to struggle in their attempt to become divine.\\n\\nAugustine lived during the fourth century of the Christian era. He saw the great Roman Empire which had been established by Caesar falling to pieces and watched the barbarians from the north gradually moving down into the empire and even toward Rome.","question":"Identify the statements which are applicable to St. Augustine and choose your answer.\\n\\na. He is the greatest Roman philosopher.\\nb. He is believed to have given an exhaustive doctrine of the Apologists.\\nc. He witnessed the great Roman Empire crumble and disintegrate.\\nd. He lived during the fourth century of the Christian Era.","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"a, c and d","B":"b, c and d","C":"a, b and d","D":"a, b, c and d"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"b, c and d","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"Statement a — the passage does not state Augustine's origin or nationality; calling him 'the greatest Roman philosopher' is not supported; FALSE. Statement b — the passage says Augustine 'worked out the theory of the Apologists most completely'; TRUE. Statement c — the passage states Augustine witnessed the Roman Empire falling to the barbarians; TRUE. Statement d — the passage states Augustine 'lived during the fourth century of the Christian era'; TRUE. Therefore b, c and d — Choice B. Choice D is too extreme because it includes statement a which is unverified in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_009","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P2","passage":"Christianity attempted to suggest that the Universe is a theatre of change and imperfection; and at the same time to teach that God is perfect and unchanging. Those men who sought to reconcile Christianity with Greek philosophy were known as Apologists. They taught that the universe contains traces of something different from pure matter and thus points to a God who is eternal, unchanging and good. This God is the first cause of everything in the Universe, the creator of the Universe. God is the eternal and abiding principle in all change, the eternal pattern which never changes. He is the unity of all forms, all ideas. Through divine emanations, He has created the world and everything in the world, in so far it is a part of God, strives to be more like God and try to return to Him. The creator fashioned the world from matter which He created out of nothing.\\n\\nOne of the greatest thinkers among these early Christian philosophers, one who worked out the theory of the Apologists most completely was Saint Augustine. God, he taught, created matter out of nothing and then created everything in the Universe. The forms which He impressed upon matter were in God's mind from the beginning of time. Indeed, God created time and space also. Thus, everything that is or ever shall be is a creation of God and must follow His laws and will.\\n\\nHere again, we see the influence of the Greeks in the belief that the Universe is the result of the coming together of matter and form.\\n\\nBut the Christian thinkers went further than the Greeks in that they attempted to account for the existence of matter. You will remember that the Greeks simply accepted matter as well as the ideas or forms as existing from the beginning. The Christians put the ideas or forms in God's mind and went on to say that God created matter out of nothing. After He had created matter, He had something upon which to impress ideas or forms.\\n\\nFurther, these Christian philosophers taught that the ideas or forms, being in the mind of God, were divine. Therefore, in so far as things are ideas or forms impressed on matter, they seek God and try to return to Him. But matter holds them back. Matter which God has created is the principle which makes it necessary for things to struggle in their attempt to become divine.\\n\\nAugustine lived during the fourth century of the Christian era. He saw the great Roman Empire which had been established by Caesar falling to pieces and watched the barbarians from the north gradually moving down into the empire and even toward Rome.","question":"According to the passage which of the following defines God?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"God is eternal, changing and good.","B":"God created matter and philosophers.","C":"God is the unity of all forms and ideas.","D":"The pattern of the world is Himself."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"God is the unity of all forms and ideas.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"out_of_scope","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage explicitly says God is 'eternal, unchanging and good'; substituting 'changing' for 'unchanging' directly contradicts the passage. Choice B is incorrect — the passage does not state that God created philosophers; this introduces a claim not made in the passage. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage states directly 'He is the unity of all forms, all ideas.' Choice D is incorrect — while God is described as 'the eternal pattern which never changes', calling 'the pattern of the world' God is an incomplete and imprecise rendering that does not constitute a definition.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E4_010","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P2","passage":"Christianity attempted to suggest that the Universe is a theatre of change and imperfection; and at the same time to teach that God is perfect and unchanging. Those men who sought to reconcile Christianity with Greek philosophy were known as Apologists. They taught that the universe contains traces of something different from pure matter and thus points to a God who is eternal, unchanging and good. This God is the first cause of everything in the Universe, the creator of the Universe. God is the eternal and abiding principle in all change, the eternal pattern which never changes. He is the unity of all forms, all ideas. Through divine emanations, He has created the world and everything in the world, in so far it is a part of God, strives to be more like God and try to return to Him. The creator fashioned the world from matter which He created out of nothing.\\n\\nOne of the greatest thinkers among these early Christian philosophers, one who worked out the theory of the Apologists most completely was Saint Augustine. God, he taught, created matter out of nothing and then created everything in the Universe. The forms which He impressed upon matter were in God's mind from the beginning of time. Indeed, God created time and space also. Thus, everything that is or ever shall be is a creation of God and must follow His laws and will.\\n\\nHere again, we see the influence of the Greeks in the belief that the Universe is the result of the coming together of matter and form.\\n\\nBut the Christian thinkers went further than the Greeks in that they attempted to account for the existence of matter. You will remember that the Greeks simply accepted matter as well as the ideas or forms as existing from the beginning. The Christians put the ideas or forms in God's mind and went on to say that God created matter out of nothing. After He had created matter, He had something upon which to impress ideas or forms.\\n\\nFurther, these Christian philosophers taught that the ideas or forms, being in the mind of God, were divine. Therefore, in so far as things are ideas or forms impressed on matter, they seek God and try to return to Him. But matter holds them back. Matter which God has created is the principle which makes it necessary for things to struggle in their attempt to become divine.\\n\\nAugustine lived during the fourth century of the Christian era. He saw the great Roman Empire which had been established by Caesar falling to pieces and watched the barbarians from the north gradually moving down into the empire and even toward Rome.","question":"According to certain Christian philosophers, though all forms are created by God,","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"they still have to struggle to prove their divinity.","B":"matter present in them impedes their attempts to return to their creator.","C":"all may not qualify to be called divine.","D":"it is surprising to find forms like barbarians that harm other forms."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"matter present in them impedes their attempts to return to their creator.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","C":"too_extreme","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — forms do not struggle to 'prove' their divinity; they are already considered divine as ideas or forms impressed on matter. The struggle is to return to God, not to establish divinity; this confuses the inference. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage explicitly states 'matter holds them back. Matter which God has created is the principle which makes it necessary for things to struggle in their attempt to become divine.' Choice C is incorrect — the passage implies all forms are divine insofar as they are ideas impressed on matter; singling out some as unqualified goes further than the passage allows. Choice D is incorrect — the reference to barbarians in the passage is historical context about Augustine's era, not a philosophical claim about forms.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_011","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P2","passage":"Christianity attempted to suggest that the Universe is a theatre of change and imperfection; and at the same time to teach that God is perfect and unchanging. Those men who sought to reconcile Christianity with Greek philosophy were known as Apologists. They taught that the universe contains traces of something different from pure matter and thus points to a God who is eternal, unchanging and good. This God is the first cause of everything in the Universe, the creator of the Universe. God is the eternal and abiding principle in all change, the eternal pattern which never changes. He is the unity of all forms, all ideas. Through divine emanations, He has created the world and everything in the world, in so far it is a part of God, strives to be more like God and try to return to Him. The creator fashioned the world from matter which He created out of nothing.\\n\\nOne of the greatest thinkers among these early Christian philosophers, one who worked out the theory of the Apologists most completely was Saint Augustine. God, he taught, created matter out of nothing and then created everything in the Universe. The forms which He impressed upon matter were in God's mind from the beginning of time. Indeed, God created time and space also. Thus, everything that is or ever shall be is a creation of God and must follow His laws and will.\\n\\nHere again, we see the influence of the Greeks in the belief that the Universe is the result of the coming together of matter and form.\\n\\nBut the Christian thinkers went further than the Greeks in that they attempted to account for the existence of matter. You will remember that the Greeks simply accepted matter as well as the ideas or forms as existing from the beginning. The Christians put the ideas or forms in God's mind and went on to say that God created matter out of nothing. After He had created matter, He had something upon which to impress ideas or forms.\\n\\nFurther, these Christian philosophers taught that the ideas or forms, being in the mind of God, were divine. Therefore, in so far as things are ideas or forms impressed on matter, they seek God and try to return to Him. But matter holds them back. Matter which God has created is the principle which makes it necessary for things to struggle in their attempt to become divine.\\n\\nAugustine lived during the fourth century of the Christian era. He saw the great Roman Empire which had been established by Caesar falling to pieces and watched the barbarians from the north gradually moving down into the empire and even toward Rome.","question":"Which of the following can best describe the relationship between the theories of Greek philosophy and the Apologists?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Compatibility","B":"Abstrusion","C":"Estrangement","D":"Tolerance"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Compatibility","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"irrelevant_comparison"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage states the Apologists 'sought to reconcile Christianity with Greek philosophy', making their relationship conciliatory and compatible. Choice B is incorrect — 'abstrusion' (difficulty in understanding) describes the content, not the relationship between the two systems. Choice C is incorrect — 'estrangement' implies antagonism; the passage describes an attempt at reconciliation, not opposition. Choice D is incorrect — tolerance implies passive co-existence; the Apologists actively synthesised the two systems, which is stronger than mere tolerance.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E4_012","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P3","passage":"Michael says he joined his Big Tech company in spring 2021 to test himself against the best software engineers in the world. Initially, the move went well. The perks also helped. But the first sign something was changing came in March 2022. Amid the economic slowdown, the free laundry service was taken away. Soon there were more cuts – the timing of free dinners for workers putting in late hours changed, conflicting with the final free shuttle scheduled to leave the New York campus. Months later, Michael was one of 11,000 employees let go in November that year, amid a broader tech slowdown and industry-wide layoffs. His next move was to leave Big Tech entirely. He now works in 'Big Finance', for a major investment bank.\\n\\nUp until recently, tech roles – particularly among major players such as Meta, Alphabet and Amazon – were often considered some of the most desirable jobs available, offering candidates six-figure salaries, lavish perks and the prospect of being at innovation's forefront. But since summer 2022, the sector has been rife with cuts, layoffs and uncertainty. A recovery feels distant. According to industry tracker Layoffs.fyi, 23,670 workers were laid off across 85 tech companies in January 2024 alone, including Microsoft, Amazon, eBay and Google. And it means some workers are beginning to look for jobs outside the sector they once competed to work in.\\n\\nBefore layoffs, tech companies were more renowned for workplace cultures that serviced employees with free benefits and premium amenities in state-of-the art offices. But gourmet chefs and meditation rooms weren't introduced just to keep workers on site for as long as possible – they were a means of attracting talent. As the economy faltered in spring 2022, rising inflation and interest rates curbed companies' growth. This initially led to hiring freezes, and then industry-wide layoffs in late 2022, which are ongoing.\\n\\nJob seekers typically want to join companies that are flourishing, in which they feel they can grow. But when layoffs are announced, that doesn't only reduce opportunities to move in the job market – it harms employers' reputations and signals uncertainty. It's not just job seekers put off by tech roles. Current employees in the industry report feeling disenchanted.\\n\\nExcitement for jobs in the tech sector will only return once the economy fully recovers, layoffs end and companies recommence hiring en masse. But for now, many tech workers are beginning to look elsewhere.","question":"Which one of the following statements, if true, would weaken the author's claims?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Tech companies are the most well positioned to keep working from home – and have even created the tools to do so.","B":"While tech jobs continue to command high salaries and offer above-average levels of flexible working, they've fallen significantly in terms of growth.","C":"History has shown that when there's job growth, that's the signal there are opportunities for job seekers.","D":"There are areas within the industry that are still very attractive to tech talent, such as AI teams among both Big Tech and start-ups."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"There are areas within the industry that are still very attractive to tech talent, such as AI teams among both Big Tech and start-ups.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"irrelevant_comparison","B":"premise_restatement","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — remote work capability does not address the passage's central claim about declining desirability of tech jobs; it is tangential. Choice B is incorrect — this actually supports the author's claim that tech jobs have lost their lustre; a student picking this mistakes a premise-restating option for a weakener. Choice C is incorrect — this is a general observation about job markets and does not specifically bear on the passage's argument about Big Tech. Choice D is CORRECT — if certain parts of the tech industry (AI teams, start-ups) remain highly attractive, the author's broad claim that tech has lost its appeal is weakened.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_013","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P3","passage":"Michael says he joined his Big Tech company in spring 2021 to test himself against the best software engineers in the world. Initially, the move went well. The perks also helped. But the first sign something was changing came in March 2022. Amid the economic slowdown, the free laundry service was taken away. Soon there were more cuts – the timing of free dinners for workers putting in late hours changed, conflicting with the final free shuttle scheduled to leave the New York campus. Months later, Michael was one of 11,000 employees let go in November that year, amid a broader tech slowdown and industry-wide layoffs. His next move was to leave Big Tech entirely. He now works in 'Big Finance', for a major investment bank.\\n\\nUp until recently, tech roles – particularly among major players such as Meta, Alphabet and Amazon – were often considered some of the most desirable jobs available, offering candidates six-figure salaries, lavish perks and the prospect of being at innovation's forefront. But since summer 2022, the sector has been rife with cuts, layoffs and uncertainty. A recovery feels distant. According to industry tracker Layoffs.fyi, 23,670 workers were laid off across 85 tech companies in January 2024 alone, including Microsoft, Amazon, eBay and Google. And it means some workers are beginning to look for jobs outside the sector they once competed to work in.\\n\\nBefore layoffs, tech companies were more renowned for workplace cultures that serviced employees with free benefits and premium amenities in state-of-the art offices. But gourmet chefs and meditation rooms weren't introduced just to keep workers on site for as long as possible – they were a means of attracting talent. As the economy faltered in spring 2022, rising inflation and interest rates curbed companies' growth. This initially led to hiring freezes, and then industry-wide layoffs in late 2022, which are ongoing.\\n\\nJob seekers typically want to join companies that are flourishing, in which they feel they can grow. But when layoffs are announced, that doesn't only reduce opportunities to move in the job market – it harms employers' reputations and signals uncertainty. It's not just job seekers put off by tech roles. Current employees in the industry report feeling disenchanted.\\n\\nExcitement for jobs in the tech sector will only return once the economy fully recovers, layoffs end and companies recommence hiring en masse. But for now, many tech workers are beginning to look elsewhere.","question":"All of the following are advanced by the author as reasons why Big Tech jobs have lost their glamour EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"cutbacks in free amenities","B":"tech companies pulling back their recruitment","C":"tech talent being in short supply","D":"cuts to perks were followed by cuts to jobs"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"tech talent being in short supply","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is a stated reason — the passage describes removal of free laundry, changed dinner timings, and loss of shuttle. Choice B is a stated reason — the passage explicitly mentions 'hiring freezes'. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage never claims tech talent is in short supply; in fact, the opposite is implied with mass layoffs flooding the market. Students picking A, B or D are restating premises that the passage does explicitly mention. Choice D is a stated reason — Michael's story shows perk cuts directly preceded mass layoffs.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E4_014","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P3","passage":"Michael says he joined his Big Tech company in spring 2021 to test himself against the best software engineers in the world. Initially, the move went well. The perks also helped. But the first sign something was changing came in March 2022. Amid the economic slowdown, the free laundry service was taken away. Soon there were more cuts – the timing of free dinners for workers putting in late hours changed, conflicting with the final free shuttle scheduled to leave the New York campus. Months later, Michael was one of 11,000 employees let go in November that year, amid a broader tech slowdown and industry-wide layoffs. His next move was to leave Big Tech entirely. He now works in 'Big Finance', for a major investment bank.\\n\\nUp until recently, tech roles – particularly among major players such as Meta, Alphabet and Amazon – were often considered some of the most desirable jobs available, offering candidates six-figure salaries, lavish perks and the prospect of being at innovation's forefront. But since summer 2022, the sector has been rife with cuts, layoffs and uncertainty. A recovery feels distant. According to industry tracker Layoffs.fyi, 23,670 workers were laid off across 85 tech companies in January 2024 alone, including Microsoft, Amazon, eBay and Google. And it means some workers are beginning to look for jobs outside the sector they once competed to work in.\\n\\nBefore layoffs, tech companies were more renowned for workplace cultures that serviced employees with free benefits and premium amenities in state-of-the art offices. But gourmet chefs and meditation rooms weren't introduced just to keep workers on site for as long as possible – they were a means of attracting talent. As the economy faltered in spring 2022, rising inflation and interest rates curbed companies' growth. This initially led to hiring freezes, and then industry-wide layoffs in late 2022, which are ongoing.\\n\\nJob seekers typically want to join companies that are flourishing, in which they feel they can grow. But when layoffs are announced, that doesn't only reduce opportunities to move in the job market – it harms employers' reputations and signals uncertainty. It's not just job seekers put off by tech roles. Current employees in the industry report feeling disenchanted.\\n\\nExcitement for jobs in the tech sector will only return once the economy fully recovers, layoffs end and companies recommence hiring en masse. But for now, many tech workers are beginning to look elsewhere.","question":"The examples of the gourmet chefs and meditation rooms are offered in the passage to show:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"that candidates in tech are attuned to employer branding and company culture.","B":"that Big Tech companies offered free food and wellness expenses with a great work-life balance.","C":"how tech corporate cafeterias serve their employees, both by the wide variety of offerings and the creative freedom allowed its chefs.","D":"that technology companies take care of the everyday details and stresses for their employees so they can be free to be productive."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"that candidates in tech are attuned to employer branding and company culture.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"irrelevant_comparison","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage states 'gourmet chefs and meditation rooms… were a means of attracting talent', directly linking these perks to employer branding and talent acquisition, which requires candidates to be responsive to such signals. Choice B is incorrect — while factually adjacent, work-life balance is not the point the passage makes; it attributes perks specifically to talent attraction, not employee wellbeing broadly. Choice C is incorrect — this reads the gourmet chefs example too literally and ignores the argumentative purpose of the example. Choice D is incorrect — productivity enablement is not the reason the passage cites for these amenities; the passage explicitly says they were for attracting talent, not for reducing employee stress.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_015","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P3","passage":"Michael says he joined his Big Tech company in spring 2021 to test himself against the best software engineers in the world. Initially, the move went well. The perks also helped. But the first sign something was changing came in March 2022. Amid the economic slowdown, the free laundry service was taken away. Soon there were more cuts – the timing of free dinners for workers putting in late hours changed, conflicting with the final free shuttle scheduled to leave the New York campus. Months later, Michael was one of 11,000 employees let go in November that year, amid a broader tech slowdown and industry-wide layoffs. His next move was to leave Big Tech entirely. He now works in 'Big Finance', for a major investment bank.\\n\\nUp until recently, tech roles – particularly among major players such as Meta, Alphabet and Amazon – were often considered some of the most desirable jobs available, offering candidates six-figure salaries, lavish perks and the prospect of being at innovation's forefront. But since summer 2022, the sector has been rife with cuts, layoffs and uncertainty. A recovery feels distant. According to industry tracker Layoffs.fyi, 23,670 workers were laid off across 85 tech companies in January 2024 alone, including Microsoft, Amazon, eBay and Google. And it means some workers are beginning to look for jobs outside the sector they once competed to work in.\\n\\nBefore layoffs, tech companies were more renowned for workplace cultures that serviced employees with free benefits and premium amenities in state-of-the art offices. But gourmet chefs and meditation rooms weren't introduced just to keep workers on site for as long as possible – they were a means of attracting talent. As the economy faltered in spring 2022, rising inflation and interest rates curbed companies' growth. This initially led to hiring freezes, and then industry-wide layoffs in late 2022, which are ongoing.\\n\\nJob seekers typically want to join companies that are flourishing, in which they feel they can grow. But when layoffs are announced, that doesn't only reduce opportunities to move in the job market – it harms employers' reputations and signals uncertainty. It's not just job seekers put off by tech roles. Current employees in the industry report feeling disenchanted.\\n\\nExcitement for jobs in the tech sector will only return once the economy fully recovers, layoffs end and companies recommence hiring en masse. But for now, many tech workers are beginning to look elsewhere.","question":"The author mentions Michael to illustrate which of the following?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Widespread layoffs may create a surprising domino effect – driving some remaining workers to voluntarily quit their jobs.","B":"Many tech workers are planning their exit strategies from their companies – and some even from the industry.","C":"Tech workers get to work on high-impact features, testing their mettle against senior developers.","D":"Tech employees had to effectively pick between free food and a free ride home."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Many tech workers are planning their exit strategies from their companies – and some even from the industry.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","C":"premise_restatement","D":"irrelevant_comparison"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — Michael was laid off, not a voluntary quitter; the domino effect on remaining workers is not Michael's story specifically. Choice B is CORRECT — Michael exemplifies the broader trend the passage describes: workers leaving not just their companies but the tech sector entirely, moving to Big Finance. Choice C is incorrect — while Michael joined to 'test himself against the best engineers', this is background detail, not the illustrative point the author is making by including him. Choice D is incorrect — the dinner/shuttle conflict is a minor detail in Michael's story, not the point the anecdote is meant to make.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E4_016","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P4","passage":"Whether in primitive tribes or the most technologically advanced 21st-century cities, human beings are born with the facility to reason. It requires rational analysis to stalk an animal, just as it does to decide which utility company offers the best deal. So why is it that we are prone to act irrationally, to be persuaded by bad arguments and led by cynical leaders? This is the question that, in so many words, Steven Pinker seeks to answer in his new book Rationality: What It Is, Why It Seems Scarce, Why It Matters.\\n\\nReason has never been as attractive as emotion, and our romantic side will always back the passionate over the rational. Yet even Romeo, as Pinker notes, citing the psychologist William James, had to employ his rational mind to find a way to overcome the obstacles placed between himself and Juliet. As Pinker points out, rationality is really just a means of getting what we want. Where things become difficult is when our brains, which have evolved to seek mental shortcuts, lead us astray – a fate that regularly visits even the sharpest of minds.\\n\\nTo illustrate this tendency, Pinker outlines some classic traps and fallacies. The favourite is the three door choice that used to feature in the American television game show Let's Make a Deal. Behind one of the doors was a car and behind the other two a goat. After the contestant selected a door, the host would open one of the other remaining two doors, revealing a goat. Then the contestant would be offered the opportunity to change her choice of door. Most contestants stayed with their original choice, assuming it was a 50-50 deal – an assumption made by even some notable mathematicians. But they were wrong. Changing to the other door improves the contestant's chance of winning the car from one in three to one in two. We struggle to compute that basic fact of probability.\\n\\nHow does a gameshow brain-teaser have any application in real life? Because we jump to wrong conclusions all the time, by relying on habit and intuition, and by fearing change. The anti-vaccine movement, says Pinker, is a case in point. It focuses on extremely rare reactions to vaccines and ignores the far more common consequences of not taking a vaccine. It's hard to argue against Pinker's own logic, yet there will always be a ghost in the machine, those urges and instincts that serve to distort reality. We are hugely more likely to meet with an accident while driving than while flying, yet this knowledge does little, if anything, to ease the fear of nervous flyers. In the end, though, rationality is all we have to prevent the world from descending into chaos or tyranny. You can't argue against it – other than by rational means, which would be self-defeating. Nonetheless, it wouldn't be rational to underestimate the lure of the irrational.","question":"Which of the following best describes the main point of the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Disentangling the mysteries of cognition and how we learn to be reasonable","B":"The defense of rational thinking and why our brains often lead us astray","C":"Making the case that humanity is growing less irrational by and large","D":"What we find irrational is often nothing more than clever inversions of logic"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"The defense of rational thinking and why our brains often lead us astray","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage is not about the mysteries of cognition or how we learn to be reasonable; it is about the tension between rationality and our tendency to go astray. Choice B is CORRECT — the passage defends rationality as essential while acknowledging that our evolved mental shortcuts regularly lead us to irrational conclusions. Choice C is incorrect — the passage states 'we jump to wrong conclusions all the time', suggesting irrationality is persistent, not diminishing; this contradicts the passage. Choice D is incorrect — the passage does not argue that irrational behaviour is secretly logical; it treats irrationality as a genuine problem.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E4_017","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P4","passage":"Whether in primitive tribes or the most technologically advanced 21st-century cities, human beings are born with the facility to reason. It requires rational analysis to stalk an animal, just as it does to decide which utility company offers the best deal. So why is it that we are prone to act irrationally, to be persuaded by bad arguments and led by cynical leaders? This is the question that, in so many words, Steven Pinker seeks to answer in his new book Rationality: What It Is, Why It Seems Scarce, Why It Matters.\\n\\nReason has never been as attractive as emotion, and our romantic side will always back the passionate over the rational. Yet even Romeo, as Pinker notes, citing the psychologist William James, had to employ his rational mind to find a way to overcome the obstacles placed between himself and Juliet. As Pinker points out, rationality is really just a means of getting what we want. Where things become difficult is when our brains, which have evolved to seek mental shortcuts, lead us astray – a fate that regularly visits even the sharpest of minds.\\n\\nTo illustrate this tendency, Pinker outlines some classic traps and fallacies. The favourite is the three door choice that used to feature in the American television game show Let's Make a Deal. Behind one of the doors was a car and behind the other two a goat. After the contestant selected a door, the host would open one of the other remaining two doors, revealing a goat. Then the contestant would be offered the opportunity to change her choice of door. Most contestants stayed with their original choice, assuming it was a 50-50 deal – an assumption made by even some notable mathematicians. But they were wrong. Changing to the other door improves the contestant's chance of winning the car from one in three to one in two. We struggle to compute that basic fact of probability.\\n\\nHow does a gameshow brain-teaser have any application in real life? Because we jump to wrong conclusions all the time, by relying on habit and intuition, and by fearing change. The anti-vaccine movement, says Pinker, is a case in point. It focuses on extremely rare reactions to vaccines and ignores the far more common consequences of not taking a vaccine. It's hard to argue against Pinker's own logic, yet there will always be a ghost in the machine, those urges and instincts that serve to distort reality. We are hugely more likely to meet with an accident while driving than while flying, yet this knowledge does little, if anything, to ease the fear of nervous flyers. In the end, though, rationality is all we have to prevent the world from descending into chaos or tyranny. You can't argue against it – other than by rational means, which would be self-defeating. Nonetheless, it wouldn't be rational to underestimate the lure of the irrational.","question":"Pinker cites Romeo to make the case that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Romeo has rationality and can therefore determine the proper course of action needed to attain his goal of getting to Juliet","B":"Romeo will climb a wall or move around it or take some other course of action that gets him to his goals","C":"our best means of getting closest to objective truth is through rational understanding","D":"even the most irrational people are capable of making rational choices"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Romeo has rationality and can therefore determine the proper course of action needed to attain his goal of getting to Juliet","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope","D":"confuses_inference_with_assumption"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — the passage states 'even Romeo… had to employ his rational mind to find a way to overcome the obstacles placed between himself and Juliet', showing rationality as the means of achieving a goal. Choice B is incorrect — the specific actions Romeo might take are not what Pinker is illustrating; this gets into vague specifics not stated in the passage. Choice C is incorrect — objective truth is not the point Pinker makes with the Romeo example; rationality here is about goal attainment, not epistemology. Choice D is incorrect — the passage does not characterise Romeo as irrational; attributing irrationality to him to then show he can be rational is an assumption not in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_018","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P4","passage":"Whether in primitive tribes or the most technologically advanced 21st-century cities, human beings are born with the facility to reason. It requires rational analysis to stalk an animal, just as it does to decide which utility company offers the best deal. So why is it that we are prone to act irrationally, to be persuaded by bad arguments and led by cynical leaders? This is the question that, in so many words, Steven Pinker seeks to answer in his new book Rationality: What It Is, Why It Seems Scarce, Why It Matters.\\n\\nReason has never been as attractive as emotion, and our romantic side will always back the passionate over the rational. Yet even Romeo, as Pinker notes, citing the psychologist William James, had to employ his rational mind to find a way to overcome the obstacles placed between himself and Juliet. As Pinker points out, rationality is really just a means of getting what we want. Where things become difficult is when our brains, which have evolved to seek mental shortcuts, lead us astray – a fate that regularly visits even the sharpest of minds.\\n\\nTo illustrate this tendency, Pinker outlines some classic traps and fallacies. The favourite is the three door choice that used to feature in the American television game show Let's Make a Deal. Behind one of the doors was a car and behind the other two a goat. After the contestant selected a door, the host would open one of the other remaining two doors, revealing a goat. Then the contestant would be offered the opportunity to change her choice of door. Most contestants stayed with their original choice, assuming it was a 50-50 deal – an assumption made by even some notable mathematicians. But they were wrong. Changing to the other door improves the contestant's chance of winning the car from one in three to one in two. We struggle to compute that basic fact of probability.\\n\\nHow does a gameshow brain-teaser have any application in real life? Because we jump to wrong conclusions all the time, by relying on habit and intuition, and by fearing change. The anti-vaccine movement, says Pinker, is a case in point. It focuses on extremely rare reactions to vaccines and ignores the far more common consequences of not taking a vaccine. It's hard to argue against Pinker's own logic, yet there will always be a ghost in the machine, those urges and instincts that serve to distort reality. We are hugely more likely to meet with an accident while driving than while flying, yet this knowledge does little, if anything, to ease the fear of nervous flyers. In the end, though, rationality is all we have to prevent the world from descending into chaos or tyranny. You can't argue against it – other than by rational means, which would be self-defeating. Nonetheless, it wouldn't be rational to underestimate the lure of the irrational.","question":"Which of the following defines 'that basic fact of probability' mentioned in the fourth paragraph of the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"When the player first makes their choice, there is a 2/3 chance that the car is behind one of the doors not chosen.","B":"The strategy of sticking with the initial choice has only a 1/3 probability of winning the car.","C":"The switching strategy has a 2/3 probability of winning the car.","D":"The probability of winning the car after switching is 1/2."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The probability of winning the car after switching is 1/2.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — while mathematically true in the standard Monty Hall problem, the passage does not state this fact; it is a correct inference but unstated in the text being tested. Choice B is incorrect — again mathematically true but not the specific fact the passage identifies as 'that basic fact of probability'. Choice C is incorrect — the passage explicitly states the probability after switching is 'one in two' (1/2), not 2/3; this contradicts the passage's own stated figure. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage states 'Changing to the other door improves the contestant's chance of winning the car from one in three to one in two', identifying 1/2 as the probability after switching as 'that basic fact of probability'.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_019","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P4","passage":"Whether in primitive tribes or the most technologically advanced 21st-century cities, human beings are born with the facility to reason. It requires rational analysis to stalk an animal, just as it does to decide which utility company offers the best deal. So why is it that we are prone to act irrationally, to be persuaded by bad arguments and led by cynical leaders? This is the question that, in so many words, Steven Pinker seeks to answer in his new book Rationality: What It Is, Why It Seems Scarce, Why It Matters.\\n\\nReason has never been as attractive as emotion, and our romantic side will always back the passionate over the rational. Yet even Romeo, as Pinker notes, citing the psychologist William James, had to employ his rational mind to find a way to overcome the obstacles placed between himself and Juliet. As Pinker points out, rationality is really just a means of getting what we want. Where things become difficult is when our brains, which have evolved to seek mental shortcuts, lead us astray – a fate that regularly visits even the sharpest of minds.\\n\\nTo illustrate this tendency, Pinker outlines some classic traps and fallacies. The favourite is the three door choice that used to feature in the American television game show Let's Make a Deal. Behind one of the doors was a car and behind the other two a goat. After the contestant selected a door, the host would open one of the other remaining two doors, revealing a goat. Then the contestant would be offered the opportunity to change her choice of door. Most contestants stayed with their original choice, assuming it was a 50-50 deal – an assumption made by even some notable mathematicians. But they were wrong. Changing to the other door improves the contestant's chance of winning the car from one in three to one in two. We struggle to compute that basic fact of probability.\\n\\nHow does a gameshow brain-teaser have any application in real life? Because we jump to wrong conclusions all the time, by relying on habit and intuition, and by fearing change. The anti-vaccine movement, says Pinker, is a case in point. It focuses on extremely rare reactions to vaccines and ignores the far more common consequences of not taking a vaccine. It's hard to argue against Pinker's own logic, yet there will always be a ghost in the machine, those urges and instincts that serve to distort reality. We are hugely more likely to meet with an accident while driving than while flying, yet this knowledge does little, if anything, to ease the fear of nervous flyers. In the end, though, rationality is all we have to prevent the world from descending into chaos or tyranny. You can't argue against it – other than by rational means, which would be self-defeating. Nonetheless, it wouldn't be rational to underestimate the lure of the irrational.","question":"All of the following are standard assumptions made in the American television game show in question EXCEPT","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The host must always open a door that was not picked by the contestant.","B":"The host must always open a door to reveal a goat and never the car.","C":"The host must indicate that each of the two unopened doors has an equal probability and that switching does not matter.","D":"The host must always offer the chance to switch between the originally chosen door and the remaining closed door."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The host must indicate that each of the two unopened doors has an equal probability and that switching does not matter.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choices A, B and D are all logical and necessary assumptions of the game show format as described — the host opens a different door, always reveals a goat, and always offers the switch. Students who pick these mistake stated or implied rules of the game for the exception. Choice C is CORRECT — the passage states that the contestant is 'offered the opportunity to change her choice', which directly contradicts any host indication that switching does not matter; in fact, the passage argues that switching is advantageous, so no such equal-probability indication is part of the show's format.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E4_020","exercise":"exercise_4","passage_id":"RC_E4_P4","passage":"Whether in primitive tribes or the most technologically advanced 21st-century cities, human beings are born with the facility to reason. It requires rational analysis to stalk an animal, just as it does to decide which utility company offers the best deal. So why is it that we are prone to act irrationally, to be persuaded by bad arguments and led by cynical leaders? This is the question that, in so many words, Steven Pinker seeks to answer in his new book Rationality: What It Is, Why It Seems Scarce, Why It Matters.\\n\\nReason has never been as attractive as emotion, and our romantic side will always back the passionate over the rational. Yet even Romeo, as Pinker notes, citing the psychologist William James, had to employ his rational mind to find a way to overcome the obstacles placed between himself and Juliet. As Pinker points out, rationality is really just a means of getting what we want. Where things become difficult is when our brains, which have evolved to seek mental shortcuts, lead us astray – a fate that regularly visits even the sharpest of minds.\\n\\nTo illustrate this tendency, Pinker outlines some classic traps and fallacies. The favourite is the three door choice that used to feature in the American television game show Let's Make a Deal. Behind one of the doors was a car and behind the other two a goat. After the contestant selected a door, the host would open one of the other remaining two doors, revealing a goat. Then the contestant would be offered the opportunity to change her choice of door. Most contestants stayed with their original choice, assuming it was a 50-50 deal – an assumption made by even some notable mathematicians. But they were wrong. Changing to the other door improves the contestant's chance of winning the car from one in three to one in two. We struggle to compute that basic fact of probability.\\n\\nHow does a gameshow brain-teaser have any application in real life? Because we jump to wrong conclusions all the time, by relying on habit and intuition, and by fearing change. The anti-vaccine movement, says Pinker, is a case in point. It focuses on extremely rare reactions to vaccines and ignores the far more common consequences of not taking a vaccine. It's hard to argue against Pinker's own logic, yet there will always be a ghost in the machine, those urges and instincts that serve to distort reality. We are hugely more likely to meet with an accident while driving than while flying, yet this knowledge does little, if anything, to ease the fear of nervous flyers. In the end, though, rationality is all we have to prevent the world from descending into chaos or tyranny. You can't argue against it – other than by rational means, which would be self-defeating. Nonetheless, it wouldn't be rational to underestimate the lure of the irrational.","question":"Which of the following statements is Pinker most likely to agree with?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Many of the things that appear irrational are actually quite rational.","B":"People are capable of being rational if the problems they are faced with are in the real world.","C":"Rationality is determining what goals a person ought to have and determining the proper course of action to attain those goals.","D":"Despite progress in overall understanding rationality, people remain just as irrational as they were."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Despite progress in overall understanding rationality, people remain just as irrational as they were.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is incorrect — the passage does not argue that irrational behaviour is secretly rational; it treats irrationality as a genuine and persistent failure mode, which contradicts this option. Choice B is incorrect — the passage does not limit irrational behaviour to abstract problems; the driving vs. flying example shows irrationality persists in real-world contexts too. Choice C is incorrect — this is a definitional statement about rationality that the passage does not specifically endorse; Pinker's argument is descriptive and normative, not definitional. Choice D is CORRECT — the passage opens by noting humans are 'born with the facility to reason' yet are 'prone to act irrationally', and closes with 'it wouldn't be rational to underestimate the lure of the irrational', suggesting persistent irrationality despite awareness.","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]`);export{e as default};
